  <section class="training-pain-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label">The expensive truth</div>
      <h2>You can keep buying leads. Your people can keep losing them.</h2>
      <p>A company can invest in better data, expensive dialers, email systems, intent tools, automation, appointment setting, and new SDR hires. None of it repairs a salesperson who loses the buyer in the first fifteen seconds.</p>
      <p>If the SDR sounds scripted, explains too much, misses the objection, asks weak questions, or books a meeting without a business reason, the problem is no longer lead generation. The problem is sales execution.</p>
      <div class="training-pain-line">The list creates the opportunity to call. The salesperson decides what happens after the buyer answers.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="training-definition-section">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The direct answer</div>
      <h2>What is SDR training?</h2>
      <p class="training-section-intro"><strong>SDR training develops the skills sales development representatives need to create and qualify early sales conversations.</strong> It should improve what the SDR researches, says, asks, hears, records, and moves forward.</p>
      <div class="training-definition-grid">
        <article><span>BEFORE THE CALL</span><h3>Prepare the reason</h3><p>Understand the account, buyer, possible problem, proof, signal, goal, and boundaries before contact begins.</p></article>
        <article><span>DURING THE CALL</span><h3>Earn the conversation</h3><p>Open naturally, communicate relevance, listen, ask, redirect, handle resistance, qualify, and propose the right next step.</p></article>
        <article><span>AFTER THE CALL</span><h3>Protect the evidence</h3><p>Record the outcome, maintain follow up, improve the message, hand off useful context, and learn from the market.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="training-symptoms-section">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Does your SDR team need training?</div>
      <h2>The numbers usually show a symptom. The calls reveal the cause.</h2>
      <div class="training-symptoms-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Immediate rejection</h3><p>Buyers shut the call down before the SDR establishes relevance, permission, or a reason to continue.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Script voice</h3><p>The words may be approved, but pacing, delivery, and sentence structure make the SDR sound automated.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Product dumping</h3><p>The rep explains features before learning whether the buyer recognizes the problem or cares about the outcome.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Objection collapse</h3><p>One version of “not interested” ends the call because the SDR cannot explore what the resistance means.</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Weak qualification</h3><p>The SDR books anyone willing to meet or interrogates the buyer so aggressively that useful interest disappears.</p></article>
        <article><span>06</span><h3>Poor meeting quality</h3><p>The calendar fills with contacts who lack fit, reason, context, authority, timing, or a clear purpose.</p></article>
        <article><span>07</span><h3>Inconsistent follow up</h3><p>Promises, referrals, later dates, voicemail, unanswered questions, and next actions are not worked properly.</p></article>
        <article><span>08</span><h3>Manager guesswork</h3><p>Coaching focuses on activity because leaders lack a shared standard for what strong call behaviour sounds like.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

