  <section class="training-quiet-section">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Quiet Power on the phone</div>
      <h2>Call control is calm, not loud.</h2>
      <div class="training-quiet-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><strong>Slow down.</strong><p>Speed often reveals nerves and makes even good language sound rehearsed.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><strong>Do not fight the objection.</strong><p>Resistance is information. Explore it before deciding whether a response is useful.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><strong>Do not explain from fear.</strong><p>More words rarely repair a buyer who does not yet understand the reason for the call.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><strong>Ask one useful question.</strong><p>A focused question creates more control than a speech filled with assumptions.</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><strong>Let silence work.</strong><p>The SDR does not need to fill every pause or rescue the buyer from thinking.</p></article>
        <article><span>06</span><strong>Exit weak calls cleanly.</strong><p>Respect the buyer, record the truth, and protect time when no useful path exists.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="training-quiet-line">The strongest SDR is not the person who talks the most. It is the person who remains useful while the conversation becomes uncomfortable.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="training-practice-section">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Practice under pressure</div>
      <h2>Rehearse the moment that keeps breaking.</h2>
      <div class="training-practice-flow">
        <article><small>SCENARIO</small><strong>Use a real buyer situation</strong><p>The rushed executive, skeptical manager, wrong contact, incumbent vendor, bad timing, no interest, price question, or unclear need.</p></article>
        <b>→</b>
        <article><small>ATTEMPT</small><strong>Let the SDR respond</strong><p>No rescue from the coach. The rep must open, listen, redirect, ask, qualify, and decide.</p></article>
        <b>→</b>
        <article><small>FEEDBACK</small><strong>Correct one useful moment</strong><p>Identify what happened, why the buyer reacted, and which behaviour or language should change.</p></article>
        <b>→</b>
        <article class="active"><small>REPETITION</small><strong>Run it again</strong><p>Practice until the stronger response feels available under pressure, not merely understood in theory.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

