  <section id="faq" class="training-faq-section">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">SDR training FAQ</div>
      <h2>Questions sales leaders ask before training an SDR or BDR team.</h2>
      <div class="training-faq-list">
        <details><summary>What is SDR training?</summary><p>SDR training develops the skills sales development representatives need to research accounts, prepare outreach, open cold calls, communicate relevance, listen, ask questions, handle objections, qualify interest, book useful meetings, record context, and follow up properly.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What should an SDR training program include?</summary><p>A practical program can include offer and buyer knowledge, prospecting, call preparation, openings, tone, pacing, value language, listening, questions, objections, qualification, appointment setting, voicemail, follow up, CRM notes, handoff, role play, call review, scorecards, and manager reinforcement.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can CallTeam train both SDRs and BDRs?</summary><p>Yes. Companies use the titles differently, but both roles can require stronger outbound prospecting, cold calling, buyer relevance, objection handling, qualification, meeting setting, follow up, and handoff. The work is scoped around the actual responsibilities.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does CallTeam provide cold calling training?</summary><p>Yes. Cold calling training can cover preparation, openings, tone, pacing, permission, relevance, listening, questions, objections, qualification, voicemail, meeting asks, call notes, and follow up. The work can use the team's real calls and selling situations.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can CallTeam write or improve our cold call scripts?</summary><p>Yes. CallTeam can review and improve cold call scripts, talk tracks, openings, value language, qualification questions, objection responses, voicemail, meeting asks, and follow up. The language is built around the company's offer, buyers, proof, and real calls.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can CallTeam review recorded SDR calls?</summary><p>Yes. Relevant recordings or transcripts can be reviewed against an agreed scorecard to identify patterns in preparation, openings, relevance, tone, listening, questions, value, objections, qualification, next steps, notes, and follow up.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How does SDR call scoring work?</summary><p>CallTeam defines the behaviour being evaluated, reviews the available call evidence, identifies strengths and gaps, and turns the findings into specific coaching priorities. The scorecard supports judgment rather than pretending every conversation can be reduced to one number.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does the training include objection handling?</summary><p>Yes. Objection handling can cover immediate rejection, no interest, bad timing, existing vendors, internal solutions, price questions, requests for information, wrong contacts, no budget, and other resistance the team encounters.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can training help SDRs sound less scripted?</summary><p>Yes. CallTeam can improve sentence structure, openings, talk track flexibility, tone, pace, listening, response paths, and the SDR's understanding of why each part of the conversation exists.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can SDR training improve meeting quality?</summary><p>Training can improve how SDRs identify account fit, role relevance, business reason, interest, timing, decision path, and meeting purpose. Better qualification and handoff can protect the sales calendar from meetings without sufficient context or value.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does CallTeam train appointment setters?</summary><p>Yes. Appointment setting training can cover account preparation, calling, relevance, qualification, meeting asks, calendar coordination, expectation setting, confirmation, useful notes, and handoff quality.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can CallTeam coach SDR managers?</summary><p>Yes. Manager coaching can include call review, scorecard calibration, coaching questions, feedback language, role play, field priorities, one to one structure, team review rhythm, and reinforcement after training.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is the training customized to our company?</summary><p>Yes. The work can be built around the company's offer, buyers, proof, sales process, scripts, calls, objections, qualification standard, CRM workflow, team experience, and commercial priorities.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can SDR training be delivered virtually?</summary><p>Yes. Virtual delivery can support distributed teams through live training, call review, role play, scorecards, individual feedback, manager sessions, and field application between meetings.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can CallTeam train international teams selling into the US?</summary><p>Yes. Training can help global teams improve US buyer language, call presence, openings, relevance, listening, objection handling, qualification, and meeting setting when the offer and market are suitable.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How long does SDR training take?</summary><p>The right duration depends on the team, roles, skill gaps, available evidence, number of modules, sales motion, and reinforcement required. A defined problem may need a focused session. Lasting behaviour change usually requires practice, field application, review, and reinforcement.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How much does SDR training cost?</summary><p>Pricing depends on team size, roles, skill gaps, call volume, review requirements, script work, number of modules, practice sessions, manager involvement, delivery format, and reinforcement period.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How is improvement measured?</summary><p>Measurement can include call scorecards, behaviour consistency, buyer response, conversation depth, objection handling, qualification quality, accepted meetings, attendance, follow up, handoff quality, opportunity creation, and manager observations.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does CallTeam use AI in SDR training?</summary><p>AI assisted tools can support transcription, pattern analysis, practice, feedback organization, and scorecards when useful. Experienced human coaching interprets context, buyer psychology, tone, relevance, judgment, and the commercial meaning of the conversation.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How is SDR training different from Sales Execution Lab?</summary><p>SDR Training Services is the role specific entry point for sales development, prospecting, cold calling, qualification, meeting setting, and early pipeline handoff. Sales Execution Lab is CallTeam's broader coaching product for SDRs, BDRs, account executives, founders, managers, discovery, deal movement, and the wider sales conversation.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do we need SDR training or outsourced SDR services?</summary><p>Choose training when your people have the capacity but need stronger skills, language, judgment, and coaching. Choose outsourced SDR services when you need CallTeam to manage the daily front of pipeline execution. Some companies need both.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can CallTeam train our team and also perform outbound work?</summary><p>Yes. The exact combination depends on the market, scope, responsibilities, access, and commercial objective. CallTeam can support team capability, managed execution, or a connected engagement when both are required.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How do we start?</summary><p>Start with a sales execution diagnostic call. CallTeam reviews the team, roles, offer, buyers, current calls, scripts, objections, qualification standard, manager capacity, and commercial priorities before recommending a training scope.</p></details>
      </div>
